medium.code:
int cur = 0, best = 0;
for (int x : a) {
    cur = Math.max(x, cur + x);
    best = Math.max(best, cur);
}
return best;

medium.why:
`best` starts at 0, which acts like a fake empty subarray. Every real subarray here has a negative sum (the best one is `[-1]`), so `Math.max(best, cur)` never replaces the 0 and the method returns `0`.

The correct answer is -1, which you get by starting from the first element:
```java
int cur = a[0], best = a[0];
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    cur = Math.max(a[i], cur + a[i]);
    best = Math.max(best, cur);
}
```
-3 (the first element) and -6 (the whole array) are real subarray sums, but neither can beat a `best` of 0.

Takeaway: initialise Kadane from `a[0]` (or `Integer.MIN_VALUE`) when the subarray must be non-empty, and always test an all-negative array.

