hard.code:
int cur = a[0], best = a[0];
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    cur = Math.max(a[i], cur * a[i]);
    best = Math.max(best, cur);
}
return best;

hard.why:
Trace: `cur` goes 2, 6, -2, 4, -1 and `best` stops at 6. The true answer is 48, the product of the whole array: -2 makes the running product -12, and the final -1 flips -48 back to +48. The code threw the -12 away because it only kept the maximum.

With products, the smallest (most negative) product ending here becomes the largest after one more negative number, so you must carry both:
```java
int hi = a[0], lo = a[0], best = a[0];
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    int x = a[i];
    if (x < 0) { int t = hi; hi = lo; lo = t; }
    hi = Math.max(x, hi * x);
    lo = Math.min(x, lo * x);
    best = Math.max(best, hi);
}
```
24 (3 · -2 · 4 · -1) and 8 (-2 · 4 · -1) are positive products too, but neither is the largest, and the buggy code never reaches them.

Takeaway: a sign flip swaps max and min, so max-product needs both running values (or the prefix/suffix product trick).

